   ],
   "notes": "Sensitivity (registered, not deciding): May-September 1.095 (1.077 to 1.114); 2006-2019 1.114 (1.102 to 1.126); SHOT plus T-SHOT 1.110; STAB dispatches, outside the chain, 1.076 (1.068 to 1.084). Exploratory: with a victim share of 0.37 (1.25 dispatches per incident times 974 incidents over 3,164 dispatches in 2023), the observed IRR implies k of about 0.64, so dispatches without a recorded victim rise with heat at about two thirds of the shootings rate. The second bridge first proposed, from heat to assault ED visits in EHDP-data, could not be built: EHDP publishes violence-related and firearm ED visits only as annual counts and rates by area (indicators 2400, 2437; SPARCS 2018-2023), with no daily or monthly series. EMS dispatches were used as the endpoint instead. Stance: rule 5, weakest link (heat-shootings or bridge-shot-ems-link).\n\nAfter the bridge referee: the test discriminates from no effect (z_null 21.7) but is weak in specificity. About 37% of SHOT dispatches are the shooting victims counted in the first link, the test refutes only if the dispatch multiplier lies outside about [-0.08, 1.09], and the stabbing contrast (IRR 1.076) would also have passed. It supports 'heat raises violence-related EMS load' more than this particular chain. Modelling non-victim dispatches directly is listed as exploratory research needed.",
   "registration_sha256": "bda255d3ebffd6189ea84b6060c0e85675e809fee6bcbdbb17a4a6281ff11099",
